  "experiment": "_022_vol_control",
  "question": "Does an inverse-volatility (risk-parity) sizing control rescue raw ranking \u2014 keep the extra targets it hits without inheriting the extra beta/drawdown (the _021 follow-up)?",
  "verdict": "No. Inverse-vol sizing does not rescue raw ranking and never beats the equal-weight champion: it softens the sp500_20 bear (-28.8%->-21.3%) but lowers return everywhere and worsens sp500_50 (bull +14.2%->+4.9%, bear -12.8%->-22.3%). On rare-event up-move cells the high-vol names ARE the return drivers, so down-weighting them sacrifices the winners. calib+equal wins return AND risk-adjusted in all 4 cells. Consistent with _012/_013/_014: no sizing scheme beats equal-weight top-3.",
  "sizing": "equal vs inverse_vol (20d realized vol, risk parity); selection: calibrated vs raw; K=3, c=1.0, rank mode",
  "equal_baselines_from": "_021 (results/backtests/_021_entry_p_threshold/rank_by/)",
